erview":{"overview":"The Rams are 5-4 through week 9 of the 2026 season and have outscored opponents 214 to 181. The offense ranks 11th in EPA per play and the defense 4th in EPA allowed, so neither side has stood out much yet. What sets them apart is the 2nd highest early-down pass rate in the league.","offense":"The Rams offense has been a little above average by EPA per play so far, and it has been much better running the ball than passing it. Their run and pass mix has stayed close to what game situations call for. Their biggest outliers in how they line up are the lowest RPO rate and a pre-snap motion rate that was among the highest in the league. Matthew Stafford has run the passing game and leaned heavily on Puka Nacua, who has drawn 28% of the targets.","defense":"The Rams have one of the best defenses in the league this season, ranking 6th or better in four of the five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area has been EPA allowed per rush, where it ranks 2nd, and its weakest has been sack rate, where it ranks 29th. Byron Young has been as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise is Myles Garrett, who has 3 sacks, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is a number of defenders in the box that ranks 12th lowest in the league.","special":"The Rams have been about average in how often they go for it on fourth down. Their kickers have made 69% of field goals, which is one of the worst in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the secondary, about 24% of the money on the books. Their offensive line was among the oldest in the league.","players":"Matthew Stafford has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 2,374 yards. Puka Nacua has been the clear top target with 28% of the looks and 715 receiving yards. Kyren Williams has handled most of the work in the backfield with 56% of the carries. On defense, Byron Young leads the team with 4.5 sacks and Quentin Lake has picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Sean McVay is calling the offense as head coach and Chris Shula is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2022 Vikings under Kevin O'Connell and the 2024 Texans under Bobby Slowik. Their defense looks most like the 2024 Dolphins and the 2023 Falcons.","trends":"The offense went from 2nd in EPA per play in 2025 to 11th so far this season, a big step back. The defense moved from 9th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 4th so far this season, a modest improvement. The biggest change in approach was number of pass rushers, which went from 4.15 to 4.37.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}